                  <msItem>
                     <title>Ambrosius, etc.</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">Bodley 843 was rebound in s. xix and contains
                        now no evidence of its provenance. Langbaine records that it was 'olim
                        monasterii Cestriae' (Bodl. MS Langbaine 6, p. 60).</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="1101/1225" notBefore="1101" notAfter="1225" calendar="#gregorian">s. xii--xiii in</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_134">Chester, Cheshire.
                        Benedictine abbey of St Werburg</orgName><p>'Fuit olim ex monasterii
                        Cestriae' according to Langbaine (Bodl. MS Langbaine 6, p. 60), who
                        catalogued the manuscript when it was Super A Art.81.</p><p>Now rebound, s.
                        xix, the inscription no doubt lost in rebinding: it was perhaps 'Ex
                        monasterio Cestrie' as in BL Royal 4 E.viii, etc. No old
                     flyleaves.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Possibly King Henry VIII, to judge from the
                     description of the binding and the probable form of the lost
                     inscription.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Queen's College, Oxford (cf. Thomas James in
                     Queen's College 62 E 6 (Opera Anselmi), against beginning of De officiis, note
                     written in 1612).</provenance>
               </history>
